===================================================================
Provenance chain, traced 2026-08-12
===================================================================

Everything above described `mlswg/mls-implementations` as a repository
with no licence file, and reasoned from there. That framing was
incomplete in a way that matters: the WG repository is a COLLECTION
POINT, so "the collection has no licence" says nothing about where the
bytes came from before they landed there. A corpus donated by one
project and re-hosted by another can carry the first project's terms
with it, and that possibility was never checked. It has been now.

WHAT WAS VERIFIED. All fifteen files under `src/data/` trace byte-exact
to `mlswg/mls-implementations` at commit
`cfd450286d1bfd9cd2519b95c80f9771f94a5b1a`. Nine are byte-identical to
upstream as fetched; the other six reproduce exactly by running the `jq`
filter commands this file already documents. Nothing was silently edited
or substituted. `passive-client-random.json` — the largest and the one
carrying the thinnest argument below — was re-fetched and `cmp`'d
independently: 1,948,393 bytes, identical.

WHO GENERATED THEM, which is what the chain question was really about:

  crypto-basics.json, treekem.json, welcome.json
      Richard Barnes (`bifurcation`), RFC 9420 editor and author of
      mlspp (Cisco, BSD-2-Clause).
  tree-validation.json, tree-operations.json, secret-tree.json,
  psk_secret.json, message-protection.json, passive-client-welcome.json,
  passive-client-handling-commit.json, passive-client-random.json
      Marta Mularczyk (`mulmarta`), a principal contributor to
      awslabs/mls-rs (Apache-2.0).
  transcript-hashes.json
      Théophile Wallez (CISPA), generated on MLS* (Inria-Prosecco,
      Apache-2.0) — PR #107.
  messages.json
      Damian Poddebniak (`duesee`), PR #82, not traceable to a named
      implementation.
  key-schedule.json, tree-math.json
      Barnes (2019) with later updates from Franziskus Kiefer (Cryspen);
      the generating tool was not established.

THE POINT OF LISTING THEM. None of these files is a verbatim copy of any
of those projects' own test suites — code search against cisco/mlspp and
awslabs/mls-rs returns zero hits for the file contents. They are original
contributions authored FOR the working group repository. So no
implementation's licence travels with the bytes, and the chain is clean
rather than laundered: there is no upstream licence we are failing to
honour, because there is no upstream licence at all.

THE IETF GRANT DOES NOT RESCUE THIS, and it is worth recording why so the
question is not reopened hopefully. Contributions to an IETF working
group are normally governed by BCP 78/79, under which Code Components are
licensed under the Revised BSD Licence — which would have replaced the
merger-doctrine reasoning with an actual grant. It does not reach here.
RFC 8874 conditions that on the repository carrying a CONTRIBUTING notice
citing the Note Well, and `mlswg/mls-implementations` has no CONTRIBUTING
file, no Note Well statement and no licence file anywhere in its tree.
The merger-doctrine argument above therefore remains the operative one.

WHERE THAT ARGUMENT IS STRONG AND WHERE IT IS THIN. It is comfortable for
the small numeric and combinatorial files: `tree-math.json` is pure index
arithmetic that this module could regenerate from RFC 9420 Appendix C
outright, and the key-schedule / secret-tree / transcript-hash / crypto
vectors are values the specification dictates, where any correct
implementation must produce the same bytes.

It is thinnest for `passive-client-random.json`. At 1.9 MB it is not a
table of spec-mandated constants but one implementation's SCENARIO DESIGN
— a randomised 200-epoch session whose shape (an UpdatePath first
reaching depth 7 only at epoch 110) reflects choices about coverage that
the RFC does not dictate. Selection and arrangement of that kind is
exactly what merger doctrine does not cover. It is also the one file this
module could not regenerate even in principle: `group.zig` returns
`error.PrivateHandshakeNotSupported` for encrypted Commits (lines 895 and
2163), so the state machine cannot drive the scenario at all, and
byte-identical reproduction would additionally need the exact PRNG seed
of whatever tool built it.

DECIDED 2026-08-12. The repository owner accepted the residual risk on that
single file, and the reasoning is recorded here rather than left implicit.
Two arguments cover it — merger doctrine weakly, implied licence from the
context of publication strongly — and neither is a written grant, so a
residue remains. It is small and, more importantly, BOUNDED: the exposure is
one test fixture in a source repository, nothing reaches a product or a
distributed binary, and the remedy if the question were ever raised is to
delete one file and lose one long-run test. That is a better trade than
paying a certain loss of 200-epoch coverage now against an uncertain claim
that the file's own author published it to invite. The other fourteen files
never needed a decision.

THE SECOND ARGUMENT, which fits the one thin case better than the first.
Everything above asks whether the vectors are COPYRIGHTABLE. That is only
half the question; the other half is whether permission was granted by the
CONTEXT OF PUBLICATION even though no licence file exists.

`mls-implementations` is the IETF MLS working group's interop repository.
Its sole purpose — stated in its own README and in the working group's
process — is that independent implementations run these vectors against
their own code to demonstrate conformance. The files were not left lying
in a personal repository and discovered; they were contributed there by
their authors specifically so that other implementations would consume
them for exactly this use.

That is the classic setting for an implied non-exclusive licence: a rights
holder who makes a work available in a forum whose only function is that
others use it in a particular way is understood to have permitted that use,
notwithstanding the absence of a written grant. Running and redistributing
the vectors as conformance data inside a conforming implementation is the
use the publication was FOR.

This matters because it inverts the strength of the two arguments across
the corpus. For the small numeric files, merger doctrine is the strong
argument and implied licence is merely additional. For
`passive-client-random.json` — where merger is thin, because a 200-epoch
randomised scenario embodies coverage choices the specification does not
dictate — implied licence is the STRONGER of the two, because that file's
scenario design is precisely what its author published it to have others
exercise. Neither argument is a licence, and both are recorded here as
reasoning rather than as permission obtained.
